"Right up to" is different from "right into", especially WRT being fatal.
(In case that running up to a monster is fatal for your character I'd
suggest to not use those commands in such cases. Your scenario is quite
meaningless applied to my way of playing the game, but YMMV, of course.)

The point of the responses in this thread was; how to avoid doing too
many keystrokes that are fatal - by using simple features to avoid the
very very dangerous perma-keypress.

I agree to the point that there are some (IMO non-serious) flaws in the
implementation of (some of) the movement commands; it could indeed be
optimized. E.g. I find it tedious to stop movement if I move away from my
own pet and the pet is at my "back side". But this suboptimal behaviour is
a price I am willingly accepting if I significantly increase my survival
rate by using those advanced movement commands.

> [snip]
> In general, I think movement in NH sucks.

To each his own opinion, of course. But what other games have so many
(IMO useful) variants to control game movement? I suspect it's impossible
to design a single semi-automatic-movement that is perfect in all cases
(and we already agreed that it could still be optimized).

I appreciate the available movement commands (you forgot the m/M, BTW,
and the counted movement) because they allow me to avoid perma-keypress
and my survival statistics got much better as I started to use it. I
stick to the suggestion to the OP to use it and gain their advantages!

